<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/bay-bridge-aerial.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/bay-bridge-aerial-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/bay-bridge-aerial.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="808" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/knowledge-base/">Knowledge base</a></li><li aria-current="page">Hurricane and Named-Storm Deductibles Around the Chesapeake Bay: What Maryland Owners Should Check</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 7 min read · October 15, 2026</p><h1>Hurricane and Named-Storm Deductibles Around the Chesapeake Bay: What Maryland Owners Should Check</h1><p class="lede">Percentage hurricane deductibles are common on the Eastern Shore, in Southern Maryland and around Annapolis. Here’s how they work and where disputes arise.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><p>If you own property on the Eastern Shore, in Southern Maryland or along the Anne Arundel shoreline, your policy may carry a Maryland hurricane deductible that works very differently from your everyday deductible. Instead of a flat dollar amount, it is often a percentage of your dwelling coverage – and it only applies when a storm meets the policy’s definition. Understanding that definition before the next tropical system arrives can save a lot of confusion afterward.</p>

<h2>How percentage deductibles work</h2>
<p>Most homeowners policies have an “all other perils” deductible, such as $1,000 or $2,500. A hurricane, named-storm or windstorm deductible replaces it for losses that meet a specific trigger. These deductibles are commonly expressed as a percentage of Coverage A, the dwelling limit, rather than of the damage amount.</p>
<h3>An illustrative example</h3>
<p>Take a hypothetical waterfront home in Calvert County with $400,000 in Coverage A and a 2% hurricane deductible. In this illustrative example, the hurricane deductible is $8,000. If wind from a qualifying storm causes $15,000 in covered damage, the policy would pay about $7,000 before any depreciation holdback. If the same damage came from an ordinary thunderstorm with a $1,000 deductible, the payment would be about $14,000. The <a href="/appraisal-calculators/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> lets you run your own numbers.</p>

<h2>What triggers the deductible</h2>
<p>Trigger language varies by insurer, and this is where most surprises happen. Common approaches include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Named storm.</strong> The deductible applies when damage is caused by a storm named by the National Hurricane Center, even if it has weakened to a tropical storm or depression by the time it reaches Maryland.</li>
<li><strong>Hurricane warning or watch.</strong> Some forms apply the deductible when a hurricane watch or warning has been issued for any part of Maryland, for a window of time before and after.</li>
<li><strong>Hurricane strength.</strong> Others require that the storm reach hurricane strength or be declared a hurricane, sometimes anywhere along its track.</li>
<li><strong>Windstorm.</strong> A few policies use a broader windstorm or wind/hail deductible that may apply to severe thunderstorms and nor’easters too.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because remnants of tropical systems often cross Maryland as rainmakers or produce tornadoes – Ida’s remnants spawned a tornado through Annapolis and Edgewater on Sept 1, 2021 – the precise trigger can decide which deductible applies. Read your declarations page and the deductible endorsement word for word.</p>

<h2>Why the Bay matters</h2>
<p>Maryland has no separate wind pool, so wind coverage near the water usually stays within the regular homeowners policy or, for owners who can’t find coverage elsewhere, the Maryland Joint Insurance Association. Insurers manage their coastal exposure partly through these percentage deductibles. Hurricane Isabel in September 2003 remains the reference point locally: its record surge in the Bay flooded Fells Point, Annapolis’ City Dock and many shoreline communities, and it is the kind of event these deductibles are designed around.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Wind and water are separate</b>A hurricane deductible applies to covered wind damage under your homeowners policy. Storm surge and tidal flooding are typically excluded from homeowners coverage and handled by a separate flood policy with its own deductible and claim process.</div>

<h2>Where disputes come up</h2>
<h3>Was the deductible triggered?</h3>
<p>If damage happened as a system was approaching or after a warning was lifted, the timing can be contested. That is generally a coverage question about the policy’s definitions, not an amount-of-loss question. Appraisal is not usually the forum for it; an attorney or the Maryland Insurance Administration can address whether the insurer applied the right deductible.</p>
<h3>Wind versus flood</h3>
<p>After a coastal storm, insurers may attribute interior damage to flooding rather than wind-driven rain or roof damage. Clear photos of roof openings, water lines and the sequence of damage help. Where the insurer accepts some wind damage but values it low, appraisal can address the amount.</p>
<h3>Is the damage above the deductible?</h3>
<p>With an $8,000 deductible, an insurer’s estimate of $7,500 means no payment. If a fuller scope – missing roof slopes, siding matching, code items – puts the loss at $14,000, the difference is the entire claim. This is where a careful estimate review and, if needed, appraisal of the amount can matter most.</p>

<h2>Steps to take before storm season</h2>
<ol>
<li>Find your hurricane or named-storm deductible on the declarations page and note whether it is a percentage or a dollar amount.</li>
<li>Read the trigger definition in the endorsement and write it down in plain language.</li>
<li>Photograph your roof, siding, windows and outbuildings now, so you have a pre-storm baseline.</li>
<li>Confirm whether you carry flood insurance and what it covers.</li>
<li>Keep receipts for shutters, tarps and other mitigation, which many policies expect you to do after a storm.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Getting a second look</h2>
<p>Maryland Insurance Appraiser reviews storm claims along the Bay and coast, including the Eastern Shore and Southern Maryland. If your dispute is about the dollar value of covered wind damage, our <a href="/property-claim-services/hurricane-noreaster-appraisal/">hurricane and nor’easter appraisal</a> service may help. For questions about whether the right deductible was applied, consult a Maryland attorney or contact the MIA. Owners around the state capital can also see our <a href="/service-locations/annapolis/">Annapolis page</a>.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/knowledge-base/western-maryland-ice-dam-claims/"><small>Guide</small><span>Ice Dam Claims in Western Maryland: Garrett and Allegany County Winter Damage Explained</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/knowledge-base/choosing-an-independent-appraiser/"><small>Guide</small><span>How to Choose an Independent Insurance Appraiser in Maryland: Questions to Ask Before You Sign</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/knowledge-base/maryland-appraisal-clause-guide/"><small>Guide</small><span>The Maryland Appraisal Clause: A Plain-English Guide for Homeowners and Property Owners</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-h">Talk to an appraiser</h2><p>A free first conversation about your estimate — and a straight answer on whether appraisal fits.</p><a class="box-tel" 